title: Debugging markers
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

When a marker doesn't render the way you expect, the engine can emit a codeblock alongside (or instead of) the normal output. This is the v1.0 successor to v0.x's `_debug` pragma attribute.

!! How to turn it on

Two layers. The static layer is a field on the marker tiddler; the per-tiddler layer is the `\debugmarker` pragma.

!!! Marker-tiddler field (always on)

Set `debug` on the marker. While that field is set, every occurrence of the marker emits a codeblock. Useful while developing the marker itself.

```
title: vocab/demo/HELLO
kind: word
open: HELLO
debug: both
```

Use this for a marker you're actively iterating on. Remove the field once the marker is shipped — the field is vocab-tiddler-scoped, so leaving it on means every wiki that activates the vocab sees the debug output.

!!! `\debugmarker` pragma (per-tiddler override)

To turn on debug for an already-shipped marker without editing it, add a `\debugmarker` pragma at the top of the tiddler:

```
\debugmarker HELLO=both

HELLO World!
---
```

The override is scoped to the tiddler that contains the pragma; other tiddlers using the same marker render normally.

!! Modes

|! Mode |! Effect |
|`pragma` |Show the `debug-string` codeblock. Default when `debug` is truthy. |
|`pragmaOnly` |Show only the `debug-string` codeblock; suppress the normal render. |
|`text` |Show the source-text codeblock alongside the normal render. |
|`textOnly` |Show only the source-text codeblock; suppress the normal render. |
|`both` |Show both codeblocks alongside the normal render. |
|`no` |Off. Also the value to use in `\debugmarker <open>=no` to override an on-by-field marker. |

!! Resolution order

Highest wins:

# `\debugmarker no` (the global off-switch for the current tiddler).
# `\debugmarker <open>=<mode>` (per-marker override in the current tiddler).
# Symbol-level `_debug` from a `_use` / `_useGlobal` resolution (legacy bridge).
# Marker-tiddler `debug` field.
# Off.

!! `debug-string` content

For `pragma`, `pragmaOnly`, and `both` modes, the codeblock content comes from the marker's `debug-string` field if set, or is auto-generated from the marker's metadata when empty. The auto-generated form looks like:

```
marker vocab/demo/HELLO open='HELLO' close='' kind='word' mode='block' element='$transclude' endString='---' srcName='body'
```

Override it by setting `debug-string` on the marker.

!! Worked example

See [[Example - Debugging a marker]]. It walks the six modes side-by-side using the demo HELLO marker, with each mode visible as both Source and Rendered.

